>> 
>> Which is why a script rather than a macro is what's needed.
> 
> What is the difference?

A macro is usually a simple playback of actions. A script allows 
more complex activities via conditionals, expression evaluation, 
loops, subroutines, etc.

>> Specifically it needs to be able to test the result of a command
>> and then conditionally continue. ISTM this may be possible for TTY
>> builds on Mac OS X with AppleScript, but not for the Qt build as
>> the contents of it's message pane aren't selectable.
>> 
>> What would worry me most about invoking a script/macro is that
>> something else that would make a player stop might not be checked
>> by the macro [like that arch-lich that teleported in while the
>> macro was searching a wall]. Short of parsing the entire map with ;
>> commands between every action (or the currently-detectable/viewable
>> area if the player doesn't have telepathy), I don't see this
>> concern being addressable.
> 
> Is this something that is a concern with something like the AppleScript?

It'd certainly bog down the processing time (or increase the 
complexity if trying to optimise that step - e.g. diffing the maps 
between moves to isolate changes).

> Because there is some event or something that triggers you to 
> stop waiting or eating when some creature comes in the room. 

That only happens in specific circumstances, of particular note: 
only for multi-turn actions [n10s is interrupted, sssssssss isn't]

If NetHack were aware of the script, and treated the script as a 
single multi-turn command, this might be enough (I suspect there 
are still circumstances where a player would prefer the script stop 
but the interrupt wouldn't normally be triggered - peaceful 
monster, and player has Stormbringer wielded); otherwise I don't 
think it would be.

> That event could be provided in the NH script language: 
> 
> if (sense_danger)
>     quit;

Indeed, if the script language was provided as part of NetHack, or 
could call NH routines itself. 

 
>> 
>> i.e. going into 'auto-pilot' kills players, invoking a macro might
>> lead to the same problem.
> 
> That gives the user incentive to create a more robust script.

True, but I suspect (for 'external' solutions at least) this would 
be rather arduous. More likely it will mean that only very limited 
things were ever done this way.

